                       Excelsior! Last Callers v1.9
                     Updated: 3/11/93 by Sean Workman
                     AmigaX BBS 503-342-2144 16.8k DS

I accept no responsibility for any damage that may occur from the use or
misuse of this program.  I've been running it for quite some time on my
system in beta testing and I've had NO harmful side effects.  Arexx
programs are fairly easy to scan through and look for potential problems.
Feel free to look through my code, but please don't modify it in any way.
I took the time to write this little utility, so if you think it's worth
using, please leave my name and BBS number on the top line of the output...

INSTALLATION:

It's simple.  I created a directory called DOORS:ELC and copied Excelsior!
Last Callers into it.  I have ELC being executed from the end of the file
called text.welcome.user.  The end of text.welcome.user looks like this:

-------------------------------< Cut Here >---------------------------------
\#0doors:Elc/Excelsior!LastCallers\
\q1\c6\@2Good \v7 \c3\v2\c6,

    \c6You have made \c3\vd \c6out of the \c3\v8 \c6total calls made to \v@.
-------------------------------< Cut Here >---------------------------------

This way it's run when a remote user logs on, but does not affect the Sysop
logging in through the special button on master.  If it didn't do this (and
you were anything like me) users would be lucky to see ANY name besides my
own since I log on and off many times a day (and if I'm testing a utility
like this, MANY MANY MANY times a day!) *B)

VARIABLES OF INTEREST:

The following variables affect the way ELC runs.  I've gone through a lot
of trouble trying to make ELC as configurable as possible.  I hope I'm
satisfying a broad range of people with the bells and whistles...

Path        : Where to look for the listing stored by ELC
File        : Actual filename of the file
Lines       : How many users to keep track of (try to keep between 5 & 20)
              NOTE: ELC is intelligent.  You can change this var at any
              time and it won't trash your data files, but if you change
              from 10 callers to 5 callers, the 5 oldest WILL be lost.
              If you change from 5 to 10, you'll keep the original 5 and
              5 'dummy' entries will be created to fill the empty space.
LST         : Long Distance States (highlight non-local states?)
LCN         : Long Distance Countries (highlight non-local countries?)
LCT         : Long Distance Cities (highlight non-local cities?)
Columns     : Use IBM-GFX chars to divide the columns? (1=yes/0=no)
LocalStates : String of 'local' states separated by spaces
LocalCountries: String of 'local' countries separated by spaces
LocalCities : String of 'local' cities separated by spaces
C0          : IPL code for color of the box outlining the listing
C1-C9       : IPL codes for columns 1-9 respectively
H2          : IPL code for highlighted city
H3          : IPL code for highlighted state
H4          : IPL code for highlighted country
T1          : IPL code for titlebar's background color
T2          : IPL code for titlebar column divider's background color
TX          : IPL code for titlebar text color

I also added the following lines to my EX.BBSMenus file:

-------------------------------< Cut Here >---------------------------------
  602,          Last,            0,           "1-32",           "\*4bbs:ex.lastcallers\"
-------------------------------< Cut Here >---------------------------------

With this line added, the command LAST will print out the Last Users chart
without actually running the program.  This is handy if you don't have your
buffer on and can't remember if your buddy called recently.

Other than that, it's set.  You don't need to worry about initializing a
data file for ELC.  If it doesn't find one, it creates one from scratch and
then adds the current user to the last entry.  Also, if you change the
number of lines to be listed, ELC will fix your datafile accordingly by
either keeping the most recent X lines or by padding the extra lines with
the default line...

USELESS INFO:

It was a real pain in the *ss to get the time formatting down.  You see if
you simply pad the size it's okay, but I wanted all the colon's to line up
when the output was listed.  I also had to deal with the two time formats
that Excelsior!  supports (24 Hour and AM/PM) Finally, after many
unsuccessful attempts, I scrapped the whole routine and started over.  And
whaddya know...  it worked great.  Anyway, the output should ALWAYS be
formatted correctly, but if it isn't, leave me mail on SkyNet (my handle is
The Trooper there.)

IDEA:

Well, it's pretty obvious that the idea for this program wasn't entirely
original.  Actually, it wasn't original at all.  I saw a utility VERY
similar to this available for <ahem> CNet.  I thought it was okay, and
started to think about it...  "Yeah, I think I could write that..." and a
couple hours later, here we go.  One major addition above the CNet program
I saw is that mine displays the number of times the person has called the
system.  I thought it more pertinant than some of the other information
(like what country the caller is from, but I've got a few international
callers on my board that call regularly so I thought I'd leave it in.)

I would like to thank the Notorious Archmage for the idea for this program,
but absolutely NO Cnet source was used in the development of this program.
I never have even seen it available for download (and I don't particularly
care to either...  I think mine's better <brag brag>).

KNOWN BUGS:

If midnight or noon occurs between the time the user logs on and the time
the script is run, the incorrect AM/PM may occur.  This will be fixed in
a future update.  My main concern with this version was a _NICE_ looking
functional version.  Remember, it is better to look good than to program
good.  And it looks MARvelous!  (Sorry Fernando, I couldn't resist...)

FUTURE FEATURES:

I'd like to add support for terminal emulations other than IBM/ANSI without
losing much speed.  People aren't going to want to sit there 30 seconds
while you write 4 different datafiles... I've got an idea though...  ;)

A new release will be done very soon that will allow compatibility for
non-ANSI terminals and will highlight non-local callers more intelligently.
I know how to fix it, but I'm hard at work beta-testing my new utility for
Excelsior! that will be very popular (I can only hope...  *B^)  )

UPDATES/PAYMENTS:

All future updates to the program are going to be available via Skynet and
my board AmigaX 503-342-2144 (16.8k DS).  People who pay the $5(US)
shareware fee will receive the latest version on a disk mailed to them.  If
you feel you would like to keep the $5, write a GOOD and USEFUL utility of
your own and send it to me.  If I use it, I'll consider you registered.
Don't send me your buddy's programs, I want something written by YOU!
(little motivational hyping there...  WRITE EXCELSIOR!-SPECIFIC
UTILITIES!) I hate sifting through mediocre CNet junk trying to find the
diamond in the rough.

